WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.500
Quentin Tarantino's ninth film, Once Upon
A Time In…

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.180
Hollywood, is packed with small details and
references to real-life events.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.820
But you don't need to be a Los Angeles native
or a true crime buff to get all the nods.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:15.960
We'll point out all the small details in Once
Upon a Time that you missed.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:19.760
Obviously, if you haven't seen the film yet,
here's your spoiler warning.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:24.690
If you caught a hint of deja vu during the
scenes in which Rick Dalton and Cliff Booth

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:29.000
are filming Bounty Law and being interviewed
about it, that wasn't a coincidence.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:33.630
In the tradition of classic western films
and television shows reusing the same set

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:39.700
to save money, the set where Bounty Law takes
place mirrors a town we saw in Django Unchained.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:44.260
Specifically, it's the place where bounty
hunter Dr. Schultz first meets Django while

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:45.940
in pursuit of his own bounty.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.680
The only question remaining is whether a character
inspired by Dr. Schulz ever appeared in an

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:53.090
episode of Bounty Law in the Tarantino-verse.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:58.710
Timothy Olyphant might be tired of acting
as the fastest gunslinger in the west, but

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:02.530
one of this movie's small details makes it
clear that Tarantino isn't sick of seeing

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:03.200
him do it.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:09.440
In 2004, Olyphant starred in Deadwood as Seth
Bullock, a man whose quickdraw is one of the

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:10.450
fastest around.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:15.500
After that, he appeared in 2011's Justified,
a modern-day adaptation of Elmore Leonard's

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.060
Fire in the Hole, which saw him playing the
part of a dedicated lawman with a ludicrously

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:20.840
fast draw.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.440
In Once Upon a Time in…

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.359
Hollywood's depiction of the real life TV
western called Lancer, Olyphant plays actor

00:01:27.359 --> 00:01:33.270
James Stacy, who on that show played Johnny
Madrid Lancer: a man who's extremely quick

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:33.940
with a gun.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.180
It's a nice wink to some of Olyphant's past
roles, but it's also just perfect casting.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.340
There's a reason audiences have been willing
to accept Olyphant as a cowboy, whether in

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.540
modern-day or period pieces, for nearly 20
years it just works.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:47.560
What're you packing?"

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.640
"You'll pay to find that out."

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.880
"Oooh, you got ice-cold water running through
your veins."

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.880
Zoë Bell has worked with Quentin Tarantino
for a long time.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:04.240
Their first collaboration was during 2003's
Kill Bill Vol. 1, when Bell worked as Uma

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:05.020
Thurman's stunt double.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:09.119
From there, Bell continued to work with the
director, doing stunts in Inglourious Basterds

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.020
and Death Proof.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:15.200
Soon, she also started performing in more
visible roles, appearing as herself in Death

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.200
Proof, along with bit roles in Django Unchained
and The Hateful Eight.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.760
In Once Upon a Time in…

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:25.910
Hollywood, Bell got a promotion to stunt coordinator,
giving her responsibility for all the action

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:27.320
that audiences see onscreen.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:32.800
In a fittingly meta-textual small detail,
Bell appears in the film as well, playing

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.180
Janet, the wife to Kurt Russell's stunt coordinator
Randy.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:40.560
Bell's actual job on-set was making sure that
stunts were performed safely without excess

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:45.900
cost to the production, so it's perfect that
her role as Janet involves furiously interrupting

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.180
Cliff and Bruce Lee's backstage brawl.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:53.300
In the world of Once Upon a Time in…Hollywood,
Rick Dalton's had quite a career.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:57.720
While he missed out on nabbing Steve McQueen's
role in The Great Escape, he might have gotten

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.280
the juicer role in 14 Fists of McClusky.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:04.560
After all, the latter film seems to be inspired
by the, quote "real events" of Inglourious

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:05.100
Basterds.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:10.310
In the clip we see, Rick plays a soldier who
blasts away at the Nazi high command with

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:14.630
a flamethrower in an obvious parody of Tarantino's
World War II flick.

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:19.100
The screen credit is more than just a wink
to one of Tarantino's other films it's also

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.650
an early hint that Once Upon a Time in…

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:25.880
Hollywood isn't going to be a basic retelling
of Sharon Tate's murder that some feared it

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:27.880
would be when the film was first announced.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:32.820
Inglourious Basterds famously ends with Hitler
burning to death in a theater alongside most

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:36.740
of the Nazi high command in a gleeful skew
of actual historical events.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:42.200
By reminding eagle-eyed viewers of that film's
climax early in his new film, Tarantino is

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:45.590
able to foreshadow Sharon Tate surviving the
Manson family.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:51.200
In the tried and true tradition of Ferris
Bueller's Day Off and nearly every superhero

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.160
movie released in the last 15 years, Once
Upon a Time in…

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:59.380
Hollywood actually has a post-credits sequence:
Rick Dalton filming a commercial for Red Apple

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:03.200
cigarettes, noting that they're not only the
best around, but have been the best since

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:04.600
the mid-1800s.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:09.420
Of course, it's all publicity for the camera,
since Rick actually hates the brand, but the

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:12.760
character would be alone in that opinion in
the Tarantino universe.

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:17.720
Red Apple cigarettes have shown up in a bunch
of Tarantino's movies both Mia Wallace and

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:22.240
Pumpkin smoke the brand in Pulp Fiction, the
Bride walks by a billboard advertising them

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:25.780
in Kill Bill, and Bob from the Hateful Eight
prefers the brand as well.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.060
"Bob enjoyed a Manzana Roja."

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.560
Red Apple isn't the only Tarantino-exclusive
brand that makes its way into the world of

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:33.880
Once Upon a Time in…

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:38.800
Hollywood Big Kahuna Burger, the fast food
franchise that's most well-known to most moviegoers

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.940
as the last meal for poor Brad in Pulp Fiction,
appears on a billboard during one of the film's

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:45.180
many driving scenes.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:48.280
Maybe they should have had Jules do an advertisement
for them instead.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.580
"This is a tasty burger."

